\pard \qc\sl0 
{\plain \b\fs28 }{\plain \b\fs20 2000 SUMMARY OF ANNUAL PRECIPITATION FOR THE SEMCOG RECORDING RAIN GAUGE NETWORK}{\plain \fs20 \par
}\pard \qj\sl0 
{\plain \fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 Annual precipitation totals over the SEMCOG network during 2000 ranged from a high 
of 47.40 inches at M-03 in southeastern Macomb County to a low of 30.99 inches at \par
}{\plain \fs20 W-35, in southeastern Wayne County (see }{\plain \b\fs20 ANNUAL 2000 }{\plain \fs20 map plus the }{\plain \b\fs20 2000 Semcog Summary 
Precipitation}{\plain \fs20  and }{\plain \b\fs20 Ranked Monthly and Annual Precipitation Amounts}{\plain \fs20  tables).  Southwest 
and east-central Livingston, most of central Oakland, northwestern Wayne, west- and 
east-central Washtenaw, and southeastern Macomb received more than 40 inches.  The 
secondary network maximum amount, 46.31", was recorded at L-09 in east-central 
Livingston, while fourteen additional stations recorded more than 40 inches.  The 
regions of lowest amounts (i.e. less than 34 inches) include: central Macomb; 
northeastern and southeastern Wayne; and one small area of southeastern Oakland.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 Compared to the long-term median amounts for each of the stations in the network with 
more than 15 years of record through 1997 (see map titled }{\plain \b\fs20 2000 ANNUAL DEPARTURE FROM 
MEDIAN}{\plain \fs20 ), 2000 departures ranged from values above the median of 13.10 inches at M-03 
in southeastern Macomb and 12.05 inches at O-13 in south-central Oakland to a value 
below the median of 0.21 inches at W-42 in northeastern Wayne.  No other station was 
below the median.  All of Washtenaw County, most of Oakland, about two-thirds of 
Macomb, and extreme western and east-central Wayne Counties were more than 6 inches 
above the median, with several of these recording more than 10 inches above the 
median.   In total area, values in 25-35% of the network exceeded median precipitation 
amounts by more than 8 inches.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 The map of }{\plain \b\fs20 ANNUAL 2000 CALCULATED PROBABILITIES}{\plain \fs20  shows the percentile ranking of each 
station\'92s annual total compared to historical data through 1997, based on a gamma 
probability distribution function.  These map values represent the decimal fraction 
of years that the annual amount at that station would have been less than or equal 
to the 2000 amount.  For example, the 0.8 isoline encloses all stations in the area 
where the annual amount would be less than or equal to that received in 2000, during 
8 years in 10.  The complement of that value, .2, means that in only 2 years in 10 
should a station be expected to record more rain than was recorded in 2000.  Care must 
be used in interpreting the values, however, because of the relatively short periods-of-record (as short as 16 years) for some stations.  The areas of extreme above-median 
values resulted in probability values over 0.9 for northwest and east-central Wayne, 
most of Washtenaw, Livingston, and Oakland, as well as the northern half and a small 
portion of southeastern of Macomb.  The highest probability values were .99+ in 
southwestern Livingston, east-central Oakland, and east- and west-central Washtenaw.  
The only area showing a below-median value, W-42 in northeastern Wayne County, had 
a probability value of 0.49.  Only two other stations, W-18 & W-35 (0.53) were lower 
than the 0.6 probability level.  Most of the network was at the 0.8 level or higher, 
with significant portions of all but Wayne County above the 0.95 level.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \par
}{\plain \fs20 Compared to 1999, the network was much wetter in 2000 (see map }{\plain \b\fs20 2000 ANNUAL CHANGE FROM 
1999 ANNUAL}{\plain \fs20 ).  Only W-27 (-0.21") in north-central Wayne County was drier than 1999; 
O-20 (0.61") in southeastern Oakland and W-35 (1.35") in southeastern Wayne County 
were the only other stations with less than 2 inches more than the 1999 totals, while  
most of the network was more than 6 inches wetter than 1999.   The greatest changes 
were:  L-05 (+14.34") in west-central Livingston; A-03 (+11.85") in west-central 
Washtenaw; M-03 (+11.42") and M-16 (+11.01") in southeastern Macomb;  O-24 (+11.13") 
in south-central Oakland; and W-19 (+11.11") in east-central Wayne.  An additional 
three stations, (one each in Washtenaw, Oakland, and Macomb Counties), recorded more 
than 10 inches above the 1999 levels.\par
}{\plain \fs20 \tab \par
}{\plain \fs20 In summary, the year 2000 was a very wet year for the residents of the five county 
area.}}